{
  "evidence": "TEXT_E: NHS investment tripled and 18-week referral-to-treatment target; neighbourhood policing, antisocial behaviour powers, Serious Organised Crime Agency; tougher immigration with a points system, biometric visas, employer penalties, faster asylum; macroeconomic stability with fiscal rules and no rise in basic/top income tax; skills, apprenticeships and FE expansion; variable HE fees with grants; free off-peak bus travel for over-60s. TEXT_D: Scrap ID cards to fund 10,000 extra police; abolish tuition fees and restore grants; replace Council Tax with Local Income Tax and introduce a 50% rate over £100,000; free personal care; UK Border Force but repeal control-order law and admit intercept evidence; strong anti-Iraq stance; convert Climate Change Levy to a Carbon Tax and no new nuclear.",
  "reasoning": "Voters in 2005 were most concerned with the NHS, crime/antisocial behaviour, immigration control, and economic stability/taxes, and TEXT_E puts these front and centre with concrete, mainstream commitments (waiting-time guarantees, neighbourhood policing and ASB powers, ID cards and a skills-based immigration system, and pledges of tax stability). TEXT_D places more emphasis on tuition-fee abolition, replacing council tax, and civil-liberties positions that, while salient to some, were less aligned with the dominant electorate-wide priorities and security preferences at the time.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_E"
}
